    "q-102704": {
      "dom_id": "step-q102704",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Given the function $f(x,y,z)=6{x}^{2}-y+{z}^{3}$, find the equation of the tangent plane to the level surface that passes  through the point where $(x,y,z)=(\\frac{1}{2},3,2).$",
      "worked_solution": "The equation of the tangent plane to the level surface $f(x,y,z)=k$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0},\\,z-{z}_{0}⟩=0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the gradient of $f(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})=(\\frac{1}{2},3,2):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\,j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(6{x}^{2}-y+{z}^{3})\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(6{x}^{2}-y+{z}^{3})\\text{ }j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(6{x}^{2}-y+{z}^{3})\\text{ }k \\\\  & =12x\\text{ }i-\\text{ }j+3{z}^{2}k \\\\ ∇\\,f(\\frac{1}{2},3,2) & =12\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\text{ }i-\\text{ }j+3(2)^{2}k \\\\  & =6\\,i-\\,j+12k \\\\  & =⟨6,-1,12⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now write down the Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the level surface, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0},\\,z-{z}_{0}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ∇f(\\frac{1}{2},3,2)\\cdot ⟨x-\\frac{1}{2},\\,y-3,\\,z-2⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨6,-1,12⟩\\cdot ⟨x-\\frac{1}{2},\\,y-3,\\,z-2⟩ & =0 \\\\ 6(x-\\frac{1}{2})-(y-3)+12(z-2) & =0 \\\\ 6x-y+12z-24 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
